  "headline": "Queen of Hill Stations",
  "short_description": "Ootacamund (Ooty), in the Nilgiri Hills, is a colonial-era hill station known for tea gardens, a toy train and cool climate.",
  "about": "Developed by the British as a summer retreat in the Nilgiri ('Blue Mountains') Hills, Ooty remains one of South India's most popular hill stations.\n\nLocated in the Nilgiri Hills of the Western Ghats at around 2,240 m elevation, in Tamil Nadu.\n\nHome to the indigenous Toda people and colonial-era botanical and tea-planting heritage.\n\nPredominantly Hindu and Christian, with several colonial-era churches.",
  "history": "Established as a hill station in the early 19th century under John Sullivan, a British administrator, and long served as the summer capital of the Madras Presidency.",
  "history_verified": false,
  "transportation": "- Coimbatore International Airport (airport, ~88 km)\n- Udagamandalam Railway Station (railway)\n- Ooty Bus Stand (bus terminal)",
  "travel_info": "Languages: Tamil, English\nBudget: mid range",
  "best_time_text": "April to June, and September to November",
  "ideal_duration_text": "2 days",
  "nearest_airport": "Coimbatore International Airport (CJB), ~88 km away",
  "nearest_railway_station": "Udagamandalam (Ooty) Railway Station, served by the UNESCO-listed Nilgiri Mountain Railway",
